# gen_fu_floor_identity: the retirement-floor check becomes an identity, and why >= let a fault through

WHAT THIS CLAIMS. A directed program carries the number its generator computed, gen_min_retired,
and the caller's plan recomputes it, so for a matched pair the two are EQUAL BY CONSTRUCTION.
The committed check compares them with >=, which a mismatched pair clears whenever the newer
program is longer. Claimed here: on one real image and two real plan values, the committed
library lets that pair through and the changed library refuses it, while the no-plan and
matched-plan paths are unchanged. NOT claimed: any simulation result.

WHY IT MATTERS, from the run that produced these numbers. A sweep built its images from an
edited generator and imported its Python from a mirror synced two days earlier, so every
program was checked against a different generator's plan. It surfaced as 57 fire-check
failures reading like a design bug. The verdict line of every one of those runs already
printed both numbers; this check is what turns them into a verdict instead of a footnote.

